  "answer": "Generally, yes—if by ‘hard to distinguish’ you mean lacking sufficient inherent capacity to distinguish one trader’s services from another’s.\n\nManual practice: A surname combined with words descriptive of the relevant services is unlikely to be sufficiently inherently adapted to distinguish. The Manual illustrates this with JONES CAR REPAIRS for motor vehicle repair and maintenance, treating the mark as a whole as not prima facie capable of distinguishing. However, the surname’s presentation matters: an unusual representation may provide distinguishing capacity that plain type would not. The nature of the services, including whether they are specialised or commonplace, must also be considered. [TMM/Part22/16/16/3; TMM/Part22/16/16/1~1]\n\nAct: Section 41(1) requires rejection where the mark is not capable of distinguishing the designated services. Section 41(2) limits that conclusion to cases satisfying subsection (3) or (4). Accordingly, the Manual’s assessment of insufficient inherent adaptation is not, by itself, a complete statutory rejection test. The supplied section 41 excerpt cuts off during subsection (4), so it does not provide that test in full. [TMA1995/s41]"
